  "live_records": {
    "q-340866": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340866",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,5),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-5,5).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-5,0)?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,5),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,5), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-5,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(3(-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
