    "q-341406": {
      "dom_id": "step-q341406",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,10),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-10,10).$ What is the value of $f(-6)?$\n\n$f(-6)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,10),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,10), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-10,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-10,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =-(-6)^{2}-2(-6)=-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
